# What goes into a variable of a module is shown or asked. Link types the variable by what it sees stored, and every function of the program reads it with no look.
# THE RED CONTROL. The lie, testing=UnseenCodeAnswersUndefined: Link takes what the shell's `id` gives for undefined, so `held` is a number or undefined, and a text is stored.
# In the first run the body of the function stores it: it printed "NaN" and went on. In the second the function is entered with an argument of another type than is written, so its slow version stores it: such a version is walked, and the door stands there too.
# OPTIONS: testing=UnseenCodeAnswersUndefined
# RUN(STORED): run-in-shell mechanisms=all-Inlining globalThis.which = 1;
# STORED: AOT: f{{*}} at {{*}}: what a variable holds is not of the type that the compiler has proven of it. It is: string. This is a bug in Bun.
# STORED-NOT: went on
# STORED: crashed with
# RUN(SLOW): run-in-shell mechanisms=all-Inlining globalThis.which = 2;
# SLOW: AOT: f{{*}} at {{*}}: what a variable holds is not of the type that the compiler has proven of it. It is: string. This is a bug in Bun.
# SLOW-NOT: went on
# SLOW: crashed with
# THE GREEN CONTROL: the same store of what fits.
# RUN(NONE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.which = 0;
# NONE: 2.5
# NONE-NEXT: went on
# NONE-NEXT: exited with 0
